00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:24.280
you’re in the right class or not.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:35.490
So, personality is a somewhat peculiar field
of study in psychology because it spans a

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:45.970
number of subdisciplines that aren’t particularly
well suited to one another. So, for example,

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:56.500
it spans clinical psychology and experimental
psychology. And it also spans an approach

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:06.670
that is associated fundamentally with single
individuals with an approach that analyzes

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:12.319
individual characteristics in large groups,
which is generally what you have to do in

00:02:12.319 --> 00:02:22.299
experimental psychology. It’s also a relatively
strange admixture of philosophy and, I would

00:02:22.299 --> 00:02:30.159
say engineering and medicine, as well as science.
And you might think of medicine as a science

00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:35.120
but it’s not, and the reason that it’s
not is because it’s concerned with the promotion

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.659
of health and health is not a scientific category.
It’s a category that can’t help but be

00:02:40.659 --> 00:02:47.129
value-laden because you have to decide what constitutes
health and what constitutes illness, or what

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:51.200
constitutes wellness and what constitutes
pathology. And there’s no way you can do

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.689
that without bringing value judgments into
the equation.

00:02:53.689 --> 00:02:59.090
It’s particularly true in psychology because
when you’re thinking about what a healthy

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:04.469
personality might be, you generally don’t
merely think in terms of the absence of pathology.

00:03:04.469 --> 00:03:09.549
You think in terms of the presence of positive
traits, right, and someone who’s fully healthy

00:03:09.549 --> 00:03:14.409
mentally and physically. While the psychologists
and especially the humanistic psychologists

00:03:14.409 --> 00:03:19.219
from the 60’s might consider someone who’s
truly psychologically healthy self-actualized,

00:03:19.219 --> 00:03:23.430
which would mean in some sense that they’ve
been able to develop the entirety of their

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:32.450
positive potential, whatever that is. So because
of these peculiarities, personality psychology

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:38.269
the course, a course in personality psychology
has to be pretty wide-ranging. And so this

00:03:38.269 --> 00:03:47.060
course is in fact wide-ranging. It brings
in elements of cultural history, elements

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:55.029
of moral philosophy, and then elements that
I would consider are almost completely biological.

00:03:55.029 --> 00:04:01.019
And it does that in order to provide you with
a multidimensional view of what it means to

00:04:01.019 --> 00:04:02.219
be a human being.

00:04:02.219 --> 00:04:06.769
I have some other principles that I abide
by while I’m teaching this course as well

00:04:06.769 --> 00:04:11.859
and one of those is that I don’t really
want to tell you about anything that I don’t

00:04:11.859 --> 00:04:17.799
you will find useful. And useful, like there’s
a difference between a fact and a useful fact,

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:21.870
right? There’s lots of facts, you can go
on Google and you can drown yourself in facts—I’m

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:28.160
sure you do that everyday. But a useful fact
to me is one that transforms the way that

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:33.389
you look at the world or the way that you
act in the world in some manner that you find

00:04:33.389 --> 00:04:41.020
beneficial. And so another way of describing
useful from that perspective is relevant.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:44.740
And so one of the principles I abide by is
I don’t really want to teach you anything

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:51.550
that isn’t relevant. And my criteria for
relevant is, first: Is it going to make a

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:56.000
difference to you personally. So are the things
that you learn immediately meaningful to how

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:01.509
you interact with the world? And then the
second is: Is there some evidence that the

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:06.330
facts that you’re going to be presented
with actually have some social utility or

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.960
some political utility or some economic utility?
So that the fact that you know them makes

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.780
a difference to the way that you act in a
way that also affects the people around you.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:26.069
And so it’s for those multiple reasons that
I brought together the material that I have

00:05:26.069 --> 00:05:26.800
brought together.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:34.650
Now that means that there are some factors
that you have to take into account before

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:40.000
you take this course. And there’s another
personality course being given at exactly

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.659
the same time, well I don’t—this semester
anyways—and so it may be that that one is

00:05:44.659 --> 00:05:48.080
more suitable for you than this one. I don’t
know, but I can tell you what this one is

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:52.289
going to be like. And so if that isn’t what
you want or if that’s not what you’re

00:05:52.289 --> 00:05:56.300
interested in then you probably shouldn’t
take this course. So the first issue is that

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:00.479
there’s a lot of reading. There isn’t
as much reading as there used to be in this

00:06:00.479 --> 00:06:06.909
course, by the way, but there’s still a
lot of reading and a lot of it’s original

00:06:06.909 --> 00:06:13.020
source material both from the personality
theorists themselves and then also original

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:17.870
source material from empirical papers. So
I would say that not only is there a lot of

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:23.430
reading, but the reading is actually quite
difficult. I think it’s useful, given the

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:28.970
way I already defined utility to you, and
so I believe that reading what I present to

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:34.360
you is going to be good for you, but if you
don’t have time to do a lot of reading then

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.560
you’re going to find this course frustrating.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:45.310
So, there’s also, because of the emphasis
on clinical theoreticians which is a major

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:50.830
part of personality theory—especially the
more classical personality theory, which by

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:54.949
the way is generally the elements of personality
theory that most students are most interested

00:06:54.949 --> 00:07:00.990
in—there’s a lot of what I would consider
philosophical material. And the reason it’s

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:06.849
philosophical is because it’s concerned
with how a person should live and that’s

00:07:06.849 --> 00:07:10.919
a much different question than what a person
is. So it’s how a person should live or

00:07:10.919 --> 00:07:16.050
perhaps how a person might transform themselves
or act in order to transform themselves so

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:22.090
that there personality is, well for lack of
a better term, better. Now, you can substitute

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:26.789
healthier for better but in a context like
the one we’re discussing the difference

00:07:26.789 --> 00:07:29.370
is trivial really.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:35.780
So, then the last thing that I would caution
you against is that I don’t lecture from

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:42.449
the reading material. So, often I’m not exactly
sure even what I’m going to tell you when

00:07:42.449 --> 00:07:50.560
I come to class. I know the material, but I
don’t what to stick to a prescribed outline;

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.430
I want to talk to you about the things that
I think are important. And so, with luck,

00:07:55.430 --> 00:08:02.210
that’ll make the lectures worth attending
and worth listening to. But, if you’re more

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:07.479
inclined from a temperamental perspective
to want a course where things are laid out

00:08:07.479 --> 00:08:11.879
in a very orderly fashion and they progress
in a linear fashion and it’s predictable

00:08:11.879 --> 00:08:17.289
and the lectures reflect the reading material,
then this is not a good course for you. And

00:08:17.289 --> 00:08:22.180
so I would presume that would be the case
if you’re orderly, and by the way, that’s

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:27.360
one of the subdivisions of the Big Five Personality
Trait Model, and orderly people tend to be

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:33.190
conscientious. But they like things cut and
dried and laid out in a linear fashion. I’m

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:40.500
not that orderly; I’m higher in openness
and openness is a creative dimension. And

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:46.660
open people tend to use relatively loose associations
when they speak and to cover a wide amount

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:52.690
of territory. And, so, some people will like
that. If you like reading literature, for

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:58.389
example, you’d probably like this course.
If you don’t, then you probably won’t.

00:08:58.389 --> 00:09:05.980
So, anyways, that’s pretty much enough for
the warnings. So, now I’ll tell you what

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:12.070
we’re going to talk about.

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:21.410
One of the things that makes human beings
unique is the fact that we’re self-conscious.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:27.600
Now, you’ll see ethologists, or people who
study animal behavior, do relatively elementary

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:33.950
tests of self-consciousness on animals. So,
one of the most famous demonstrations of such

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:38.589
self-consciousness is the mirror test. And
so in the mirror test what you essentially

00:09:38.589 --> 00:09:44.870
do is you take an animals, maybe a chimp or
maybe a three year old child, and you put

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:49.440
a lipstick mark on their forehead or on their
nose and then you show them a reflection of

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:55.010
themselves in the mirror. And if they wipe
off or gesture towards the lipstick mark then

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:59.600
can make the presumption that they understand
that it’s them that’s being reflected

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:04.470
in the mirror, or at least they understand
that well enough so that they can use the

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:11.130
mirror as a guide to action with their hand
or whatever it is that they happen to be pointing

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:16.529
with and the reflection. Now, dolphins seem
to be able to manage that, there’s some

00:10:16.529 --> 00:10:21.160
members of the corvid family, so those are
basically crows and ravens—those things

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:26.820
are really smart and they seem to possess
rudimentary self-consciousness—I said dolphins,

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:31.690
cetaceans in general, so whales—although
I don’t know if all whales are self-conscious

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:37.139
because it’s for example difficult to get
a mirror big enough to show a blue whale—human

00:10:37.139 --> 00:10:47.589
beings, and that’s about it. Now, I don’t
think that’s a very good definition of self-consciousness

00:10:47.589 --> 00:10:51.420
really, because the mere fact that you can
recognize yourself, it’s also not fair to

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:56.430
some animals like dogs, right, because dogs
would need a smell mirror really, because,

00:10:56.430 --> 00:11:03.930
and most animals are very very smell predicated.
Most of the brain is structured with the olfactory

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:08.320
system as its core, unlike us really because
our brains are structured with the visual

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:12.660
system at the core and that makes us rather
peculiar. So it’s a little unfair to animals

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:17.410
like dogs or any rats that rely primarily
on olfaction to orient themselves in the world.

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:21.220
But the thing is I don’t think that’s
really a very good definition of self-consciousness;

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:22.839
it’s very rudimentary.

00:11:22.839 --> 00:11:27.870
You might say well that’s the first sort
of step to being self-conscious because if

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:32.019
you’re fully self-conscious I think you
need to two more things. You need the capacity

00:11:32.019 --> 00:11:38.019
to imitate, which is a much more important
capacity than people generally realize. In

00:11:38.019 --> 00:11:45.139
fact, I would say it’s of equivalent importance—it’s
of equivalent importance to language. In fact,

00:11:45.139 --> 00:11:49.870
I’m not even sure that you can use language
if you can’t imitate. First of all, obviously

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:54.790
all the words you use are also the words that
other people use. Otherwise, you’re not

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:59.339
going to be very comprehensible. So, even the
mere fact that you do speak means that you’re

00:11:59.339 --> 00:12:05.320
relying on imitation to get your point across.
So, you need imitation and the reason you need

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:10.560
imitation is because once you can imitate,
you can use your body as a representational

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:16.089
structure to represent the world and other
people. You can even use your body as a representational

00:12:16.089 --> 00:12:19.850
structure to represent yourself, which you
might do, for example, if you’re telling

00:12:19.850 --> 00:12:26.070
a joke at a dinner party and then you, you
know, act out a self-parody and everyone laughs.

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:31.880
So, and then with regards to language, well
language obviously enables you to build sophisticated

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:36.610
models of the world that are well-articulated,
to exchange those with other people, and to

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:37.149
think.

00:12:37.149 --> 00:12:42.550
Ok so, in order to be self-conscious you have
to be able to recognize that you exist in

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:48.329
this physical envelope, at least here and
now; you need to be able to imitate, because

00:12:48.329 --> 00:12:52.949
that’s partly how you come to understand
yourself and other creatures like you, which

00:12:52.949 --> 00:12:57.769
is a very important part of being self-conscious,
right—I mean everything I learn about any

00:12:57.769 --> 00:13:02.350
of you is also going help me understand the
sort of creature that I am, whether that’s

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:09.290
for good or for evil—and then with language,
well not only can we articulate our representations

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:14.050
about others in the world, but we can also
store those articulated representations over

00:13:14.050 --> 00:13:20.199
very very very very long spans of time. And
we do that partly in ritual, which is the

00:13:20.199 --> 00:13:25.279
acting out of a representation, and we do
that partly with regards to stories, which

00:13:25.279 --> 00:13:31.160
are in some sense an articulated representation
of an acting out, right, because you can think

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:38.079
of a story as a drama. So a drama is behavior
representing behavior and then a story is

00:13:38.079 --> 00:13:45.500
an articulated representation of a drama.
Now we have very old rituals and we have very

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:51.990
very old stories. And so, part of what we
do in this course is we go back to as close

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:57.329
to the beginning as we can possibly manage
to start to understand how people have understood

00:13:57.329 --> 00:14:03.290
themselves and represented themselves across
the span of human history. And we’re doing

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:08.069
that because we want to be able to put our
current knowledge in some kind of historical

00:14:08.069 --> 00:14:08.589
context.

00:14:08.589 --> 00:14:14.670
And part of the reason for that is, well one
of the things that characterizes a human personality

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:19.829
is that it’s a historical phenomena. You
know I’m sure you’ve heard in many classes

00:14:19.829 --> 00:14:26.029
that much of what makes up your personality
is a cultural construct. Well, whether or

00:14:26.029 --> 00:14:30.009
not much of it is or not isn’t really the
issue. Certainly some of it is, and an important

00:14:30.009 --> 00:14:34.690
part—you’re a cultural creature. And that
means that you have emerged from a tradition

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:41.380
that has been shaped in any number of ways
by an extraordinary lengthy historical past—well

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:48.410
historical and biological. And so what that
means in part is that if you don’t understand

00:14:48.410 --> 00:14:55.490
your history, and that’s your history as
it matters, not necessarily some collection

00:14:55.490 --> 00:15:01.690
of facts, then you don’t understand yourself.
So, you know, human beings in their self-conscious

00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:08.750
form, we don’t really know how old we are.
More or less biologically or genetically identical

00:15:08.750 --> 00:15:15.250
creatures to us existed at least 150 000 years
ago but we seem to have discovered fire and

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:20.509
were able to master it maybe 2 million years
ago. You know and that requires a fair bit

00:15:20.509 --> 00:15:30.920
of intelligence. And there are artifacts that
indicate a fairly high level of cultural capacity

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:36.589
that are at least 50 000 years old. So, we
can’t really say when we got fully self-conscious,

00:15:36.589 --> 00:15:40.810
and perhaps we might also say that we’re
not even fully self-conscious yet because

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:44.089
you know, really, what the hell do you know
about yourself. You know, you’re really

00:15:44.089 --> 00:15:49.360
really really really complicated. And at best
you have a partial model of who you are and

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:52.899
that thing fails all the tail, which as you
can tell because you’re always doing things

00:15:52.899 --> 00:16:00.100
that surprise you and often not in a particularly
positive way. So, there’s no end of mystery.

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:07.410
Anyways, the upshot of all of this is that
we’re going to establish the context first

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:12.410
that’s a historical context and I want to
do that two ways. The first thing I want to

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:19.129
do is to talk to you about mythological representations
of personality. And that’s more important

00:16:19.129 --> 00:16:26.449
than you might think. In fact, it’s probably
more important than you do think. By myth,

00:16:26.449 --> 00:16:35.690
what I mean is the representations of human
beings in extremely long-lasting and persistent

00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:41.800
stories. And so some of those are the stories
that are found in various religious traditions,

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:45.149
but there’s oral traditions that surround
those religious traditions, you know assuming

00:16:45.149 --> 00:16:49.589
that the religious traditions are written
down; and there’s fairy tales and that sort

00:16:49.589 --> 00:16:54.730
of thing; and standard narratives that people
used to guide themselves as the moved through

00:16:54.730 --> 00:17:02.019
the world. And most of the clinical theories
that we’re going to discuss, so Freud’s

00:17:02.019 --> 00:17:05.870
theory and Jung’s theory and the theory
of the Existentialists and the Phenomenologists

00:17:05.870 --> 00:17:11.520
and the Humanists and so forth, are variants
of those mythological stories.

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:17.559
Now, part of the reason for that is that Nietzsche
said once that a philosopher was seldom anything

00:17:17.559 --> 00:17:25.559
other than the unconscious advocate of their
cultural worldview. So, what he meant by that

00:17:25.559 --> 00:17:30.950
was you already come to the scene with a set
of presuppositions. Now, some of those are

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:35.039
embedded in your behavior. So, for example,
when you walk—when you attend a funeral

00:17:35.039 --> 00:17:38.919
you know how to behavior. Generally, you’re
quiet and solemn. You might not be able to

00:17:38.919 --> 00:17:45.000
explicate the rules a person should follow
at a funeral, but you can do it. Now, you could

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:49.350
perhaps articulate them if pressed, but it’s
not like you’re following those rules when

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:53.440
you go to a funeral; you just know what to
do. If someone pressed you, you could turn

00:17:53.440 --> 00:17:58.700
it into a list of rules but it’s really
more encoded in you’re actual behavior.

00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:03.030
You just automatically know what to do. You
can tell, you know, whenever you interact

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:08.280
with someone who’s really socially fluent,
you can tell that they’re not consciously

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:13.480
processing the way that they’re acting—otherwise
they’re self-conscious and awkward. They’re

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:18.490
very fluid. It’s automatized behavior and
it’s coded right into them. So, if you’re

00:18:18.490 --> 00:18:22.429
a great philosopher, according to Nietzsche
at least, what you do is you take a look at

00:18:22.429 --> 00:18:27.559
how you act and what you think and then you
say “Well this is what it’s like.” It’s

00:18:27.559 --> 00:18:32.460
not so much that you’re inventing new ideas;
it’s that you’re articulating the nature

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:34.640
of structures that are already there.

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:40.570
So, you can imagine, for example, imagine that
you’re particularly interested in wolves.

00:18:40.570 --> 00:18:44.350
And so you go out and you study some wolves
and one of the things you see is that the

00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:49.780
wolves have a dominance hierarchy. So, there’s
a leader and then there is struggle in the

00:18:49.780 --> 00:18:54.929
pack to see who’s going to be the dominant
leader. Now, there’s a couple problems with

00:18:54.929 --> 00:19:00.730
that which is that, you know if Wolf A and
Wolf B fight and they’re pretty evenly matched

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:04.530
and they tear each other to shreds, then Wolf
C is going to come in and pound both of them

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:10.200
and that’s not a particularly useful strategy
for Wolf A or for Wolf B. Plus then the pack

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:15.600
loses the power of Wolf A and Wolf B, so that’s
hardly useful at all. So, what’s happened

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:20.450
is that wolves have evolved dominance and
submission strategies so that they can figure

00:19:20.450 --> 00:19:25.340
out who is going to lead and who is going
to follow without having to engage in the

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:29.390
kind of physical combat that’s going to
result in damage.

00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:33.740
And so usually what happens is that two wolves
face off—you’ve probably seen cats do

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:37.400
this when they encounter each other you know
out on the street—the first thing they do

00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:41.549
is sort of turn sideways and puff themselves
up, right, and they dance back and forth;

00:19:41.549 --> 00:19:45.210
it’s quite comical. But what the cat’s
trying to do is look big and it’s supposed

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:50.059
to—it’s trying to make the other cat nervous
enough to leave. And so what wolves do is

00:19:50.059 --> 00:19:53.470
roughly the same thing. They puff out so they
look big and they make all sorts of ferocious

00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:59.330
noises and they threaten each other and usually
what happens is one wolf will lose his nerve,

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:04.130
flop down, roll over, and show his throat.
And that basically means something like “I’m

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:08.730
useless and, you know, and pathetic and you
can tear up my throat if you want because

00:20:08.730 --> 00:20:12.950
of that,” and the other wolf thinks “Yeah,
you are pretty useless and pathetic, but you

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:16.289
know, we’re going to need you tomorrow to
hunt down a deer, so you know, you can get

00:20:16.289 --> 00:20:21.620
up and protect your miserable self and that’ll
be okay.” Now, if you were watching that,

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:26.520
you might say, “Wolves follow rules,”
but of course they don’t; that’s just

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:31.370
how wolves act. When you describe what the
wolves are doing then you turn it into a rule,

00:20:31.370 --> 00:20:34.240
but it wasn’t a rule to begin with; it was
just a pattern of behavior.

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:41.159
Now human beings are like that. We’re chalked
full of patterns of behavior. And they’re

00:20:41.159 --> 00:20:45.690
the consequence of our biology, which is something
we’ll talk about in depth in the last half

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:49.890
and the course, and they’re a consequence
of our culture. And they’re built into our

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:54.360
bodies. Like every time you interact with
another person, they’re telling you how

00:20:54.360 --> 00:21:00.230
to behave, right? “Don’t be too obtuse.”
“Try to be a little bit witty.” “Have

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:03.740
something interesting to say.” “Don’t
be too annoying.” “If you’re going to

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:08.620
say something funny make sure it’s funny.”
“Take turns while you talk.” “Play nicely.”

00:21:08.620 --> 00:21:14.650
“Take your turn.” “Don’t eat all the
bread.” Etc, etc. You know, and so if you

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:21.140
don’t abide by those rules then people lift
their eyebrow at you. Or they don’t smile

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:24.409
at your jokes. Or they don’t look at you.
Or they roll their eyes when you come in,

00:21:24.409 --> 00:21:30.750
which is really not a good sign by the way.
And you get punished severely for it. And

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:34.529
then if you do act like they would like you
to act, you know, then people smile at you

00:21:34.529 --> 00:21:38.270
and they invite you to their place for dinner
and, you know, maybe you have an intimate

00:21:38.270 --> 00:21:42.630
relationship and some people that like you.
And so we’re exchanging information about

00:21:42.630 --> 00:21:48.570
how we should manifest ourselves in our bodies
with every possible interaction. And what

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:51.100
that means is that shapes the way that we
act.

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:56.070
Now, the problem is—or partly is—is that
you don’t really understand the consequences

00:21:56.070 --> 00:22:00.419
of that. Like, you can act it out more or
less depending on your social skill and the

00:22:00.419 --> 00:22:05.260
degree to which you’re been exposed to other
people, but that doesn’t mean that you’re

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:09.340
conscious—that you can consciously articulate
all the rules that underlie that and if you

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:15.640
think that maybe you can well fine. You know,
imagine a well-behaved four-year-old. You

00:22:15.640 --> 00:22:21.179
know, by four, you’ve got a lot of the social
rules down because by four you can play with

00:22:21.179 --> 00:22:25.720
others. If you’re ever going to be able
to you can already do it by the time you’re

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:30.309
four. If you can’t by the time you’re
four you are in serious trouble because other

00:22:30.309 --> 00:22:33.730
kids won’t play with you and then they won’t
teach you how to act and then you stay immature

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:40.230
and isolated and, you know, off in you’re
little corner dejected and bitter for the

00:22:40.230 --> 00:22:44.220
rest of your life. Now, you know, you might
that that’s a little rough but it’s not;

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:46.429
that’s what the data indicate.

00:22:46.429 --> 00:22:56.799
So, anyways, what this means in part is, well,
we’ve been shaping how all of us behave

00:22:56.799 --> 00:23:04.240
as long as we’ve been social. Now you might
say “Well how long have we been social?”

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:12.630
Well, crustaceans are social. So, lobsters,
for example, organize themselves into dominance

00:23:12.630 --> 00:23:18.600
hierarchies and they modify each others’
behavior. And that’s 400 million years.

00:23:18.600 --> 00:23:28.580
So, we’ve been social and living in groups
since our ancestors departed on the evolutionary

00:23:28.580 --> 00:23:33.570
timescale from the ancestors of lobsters.
So, that’s a very very very long time. There

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:41.289
weren’t any trees around back then. So, social
life is older than trees. So, you can be sure

00:23:41.289 --> 00:23:46.399
that you’re adapted to it in a major way.
So, it’s built into your biology but then

00:23:46.399 --> 00:23:53.029
there’s the historic element of it too.
And it’s quite—we’re quite flexible

00:23:53.029 --> 00:23:54.090
from a cultural perspective.

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:59.380
So, there’s an example: this guy named Robert
Sapolski, who I really like; he’s a great

00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:06.580
scientist. He went off to Africa to study
baboons. And baboons are not pleasant, generally

00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:10.840
speaking. They fight a lot; they’re bullies.
They bite. They can really bite. A baboon,

00:24:10.840 --> 00:24:16.570
like a predatory cat will think twice about
taking on a baboon. They’ve got major teeth

00:24:16.570 --> 00:24:21.860
and their temperament is not particularly
positive. Sapolski went and studied a group

00:24:21.860 --> 00:24:27.850
of baboons and they were engaged in typical
baboon behavior, grasping each other all the

00:24:27.850 --> 00:24:31.159
time and bullying each other. And they were
all stressed out because—Sapolski knew that

00:24:31.159 --> 00:24:35.520
because he measured their levels of stress
and testosterone and so forth. And so it was

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:41.299
a pretty miserable baboon existence, but then
what happened was that the baboons went to

00:24:41.299 --> 00:24:47.070
feed somewhere that I don’t remember and
they—a lot of them contracted tuberculosis.

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:51.840
And it turned out that almost all the hyper
aggressive males got tuberculosis and died

00:24:51.840 --> 00:24:56.380
and all that was left was the beta males,
roughly speaking. And then the whole baboon

00:24:56.380 --> 00:25:00.440
culture transformed so that it because much
more agreeable and much more cooperative.

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:05.990
And then when aggressive baboons would move
into that territory, you know singly, on their

00:25:05.990 --> 00:25:10.840
own, then they’d get all placid and calm
too. So, it’s interesting because obviously

00:25:10.840 --> 00:25:16.760
baboons aren’t social to the degree we are
but it does indicate that even at the advanced

00:25:16.760 --> 00:25:21.450
primate level—you know their brains are
pretty big but not as big as ours—that cultural

00:25:21.450 --> 00:25:26.409
transmission of behavior expectation can be
quite powerful and it can transform quite

00:25:26.409 --> 00:25:31.710
rapidly. So, anyways we’ve been shaping
each others’ behavior for a very very very

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:38.880
very long time. And a lot of that’s encoding
deeply in biology and in culture.

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:43.649
Now biologically, and we’ll talk about this
a lot when we get to the biological section,

00:25:43.649 --> 00:25:49.620
one of the ways you can know how social you
are is by how hurt and ashamed you get when

00:25:49.620 --> 00:25:53.799
you make a serious social error. You know,
so if you’re at a party and you make a fool

00:25:53.799 --> 00:25:57.830
of yourself, you know, depending on your level
of neuroticism—which is also a Big Five

00:25:57.830 --> 00:26:02.510
Trait that indexes how sensitive you are to
negative emotion—you might obsess about

00:26:02.510 --> 00:26:05.830
what an idiot you are for the next three or
four months, or maybe even for the next five

00:26:05.830 --> 00:26:12.740
years. You know, people do not like social
exclusion. You know, and you have a little

00:26:12.740 --> 00:26:17.320
counter that’s more or less built into the
back of your brain that roughly, first of

00:26:17.320 --> 00:26:21.159
all it estimates how successful you are—there’s
an estimation that actually seems to occur

00:26:21.159 --> 00:26:27.010
right at birth where when part of your neuroticism
levels are set. And then that can be modified

00:26:27.010 --> 00:26:32.450
to some degree by learning. You know, and
if you’re a top baboon, well you’re going

00:26:32.450 --> 00:26:36.799
to experience less negative emotion and more
satisfaction than you are if you’re a bottom

00:26:36.799 --> 00:26:43.039
baboon and it’s exactly the same with people.
So, you know, we like to be climbing up dominance

00:26:43.039 --> 00:26:45.860
hierarchies and we like to be near the top
of dominance hierarchies. And if the little

00:26:45.860 --> 00:26:49.710
counter at the back of your brain notices
that, you know, you’re doing a good job

00:26:49.710 --> 00:26:54.480
of climbing towards the top of relevant dominance
hierarchy then its going to produce more serotonin

00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:58.309
and you’re not going to be in as much pain,
you’re not going to be as depressed, you’re

00:26:58.309 --> 00:27:03.409
not going to be as anxious, you’re not going
to be stressed, and so forth. So there’s

00:27:03.409 --> 00:27:08.149
a biological basis for our social being and
it’s extraordinarily important to us how

00:27:08.149 --> 00:27:09.960
we fit into social groups.

00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:17.470
Ok, so we’ve also been trying to figure
out what the hell we are, who we are, for

00:27:17.470 --> 00:27:23.100
thousands and thousands of years, partly as
a consequence of imitation, you know, because

00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:27.140
I can act you out and that’ll sort of help
me understand what you’re like; and partly

00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:32.669
as a consequence of articulated communication.
A lot of that’s storytelling. Now people

00:27:32.669 --> 00:27:38.909
have been telling stories for we have no idea
how long. I mean, if you go and visit peoples

00:27:38.909 --> 00:27:43.649
that have no written language and who have
been isolated from the rest of human society

00:27:43.649 --> 00:27:48.260
for let’s say tens of thousands of years—and
such people still exist—you find that they

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:53.919
tell stories, they dance, they act each other
out, you know. So, we know certainly that the

00:27:53.919 --> 00:27:58.820
propensity to tell stories and to engage in
ritual behavior is far older than our ability

00:27:58.820 --> 00:28:04.600
to write. I suspect you can push it back at
least 50 000 years, although I suspect it’s

00:28:04.600 --> 00:28:12.480
a lot older than that. And so, what’s happened
is that across those timescales, we’ve started

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:17.940
to tell stories about one another that represent
how we act. And then you might think well

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:21.190
some of those stories are interesting and
memorable and some of them aren’t and then

00:28:21.190 --> 00:28:25.830
we tend to remember the interesting and memorable
ones and pass them on.

00:28:25.830 --> 00:28:31.640
And so you can also imagine human beings aggregating
together in groups of thousands and thousands

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:36.590
of people over thousands and thousands of
years telling their stories to one another

00:28:36.590 --> 00:28:41.429
and hammering those stories into some sort
of mutually acceptable and comprehensible

00:28:41.429 --> 00:28:47.850
form. And so, for example one example of that
is that if you look at the creation stories—the

00:28:47.850 --> 00:28:52.299
creation story that say occurs in Genesis
in the Old Testament—it’s a member of

00:28:52.299 --> 00:28:58.789
a class of creation stories that characterized
Middle Eastern mythology and it seems to have

00:28:58.789 --> 00:29:03.990
arisen out of the organization of separate
Middle Eastern tribes thousands and thousands

00:29:03.990 --> 00:29:10.320
of years ago, each with their own traditions,
into a relatively homogenous story that characterized

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:19.490
a large group of people’s central beliefs.
So, we have information about how we act from

00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:23.990
the representations that we’ve made of those
actions that we’ve passed forward and a

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:30.190
lot of those take the form of myths—religious
stories. And, so, part of what we’re going

00:29:30.190 --> 00:29:37.710
to do is we’re going to begin the course
with two foundation-establishing themes. One

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:45.179
has to do with the structure of mythological
representations and the second has to do with

00:29:45.179 --> 00:29:52.230
a set of rituals and processes and beliefs
that have been identified more or less worldwide

00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:54.870
that have to do with shamanic transformation.

00:29:54.870 --> 00:29:59.779
Now, the title of this course is “Personality
and its Transformations,” and the reason

00:29:59.779 --> 00:30:05.649
it’s titled that way is because you aren’t
just who you are. You’re also something

00:30:05.649 --> 00:30:10.610
that changes all the time. You’re more like
a river than like a rock. You’re constantly

00:30:10.610 --> 00:30:17.000
transforming. What’s the name of? The physicist
Erwin Schrödinger thought that people were

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:22.510
dissipative structures. It’s a very interesting
idea. So, a dissipative structure is a structure

00:30:22.510 --> 00:30:28.750
that maintains its structural integrity while
its elements transform completely. So you

00:30:28.750 --> 00:30:31.720
know when you let the water out of a sink
you get a whirlpool, right? And the whirlpool

00:30:31.720 --> 00:30:37.179
is a fairly stable entity, but the water that
the whirlpool is made of is constantly changing.

00:30:37.179 --> 00:30:41.340
That’s a dissipative structure. Well, that’s
what you’re like. You maintain your form

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:47.159
across time but your elements are constantly
transforming. And people are very very transformative

00:30:47.159 --> 00:30:53.840
creatures. You know, we can, unlike animals
really because animals are sort of what they

00:30:53.840 --> 00:30:57.940
are and that’s it. I mean they have some
capacity to learn but not much. Your typical

00:30:57.940 --> 00:31:03.289
beaver is acting more or less today like,
you know, his great great great great grandfather

00:31:03.289 --> 00:31:10.980
was acting 5000 years ago. There’s no real
accretion of culture, whereas with human being,

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:16.779
we transform like mad. I mean we do that culturally,
things aren’t much—there’s so many things,

00:31:16.779 --> 00:31:20.029
for example, that are different now than there
were twenty years ago that it’s almost impossible

00:31:20.029 --> 00:31:20.669
to imagine.

00:31:20.669 --> 00:31:25.389
And then, so we transform on a cultural scale
quite rapidly but we also do that individually.

00:31:25.389 --> 00:31:30.789
I mean you guys have all just made the transition
to university, just roughly speaking. You

00:31:30.789 --> 00:31:35.210
know, I’m sure that you’re like you were
in some ways when you were at high school,

00:31:35.210 --> 00:31:38.960
but well hopefully you’re better than you
were in high school a whole bunch of ways

00:31:38.960 --> 00:31:44.000
as well. You should be more mature but your
personality may have transformed substantially.

00:31:44.000 --> 00:31:49.580
You know, and with any luck you’re not done
with your sequences of transformations. You

00:31:49.580 --> 00:31:52.909
know, that’s going to be something that
characterizes you for the rest of your life

00:31:52.909 --> 00:31:58.070
insofar as you’re a dynamic person, your
eyes are open, you pay attention, and you

00:31:58.070 --> 00:32:03.230
can learn. And so one of the things you might
say is that the most stable thing there is

00:32:03.230 --> 00:32:07.960
about you in personality is its capacity to
transform. And that’s really something that’s

00:32:07.960 --> 00:32:11.940
worth knowing, you know, because one of the
things I can tell you about the great myths

00:32:11.940 --> 00:32:15.960
of mankind—there’s lots of them and they
have different themes—but one of the most

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:23.980
powerful themes is the way you get yourself
out of trouble is to transform your personality.

00:32:23.980 --> 00:32:28.389
Now you think, you kind of do that every time
you learn something, right, because you learn

00:32:28.389 --> 00:32:31.610
something and you’re a little different
than you were before. Hopefully different

00:32:31.610 --> 00:32:35.700
in a significant way and hopefully different
in a way that helps you solve problems that

00:32:35.700 --> 00:32:41.340
you couldn’t solve before. So, you could
say that one of the leitmotifs of being human

00:32:41.340 --> 00:32:45.519
is, “well if I encounter an obstacle I can
just transform and I can figure out some way

00:32:45.519 --> 00:32:50.389
of getting around it,” and that’s something
that makes us quite remarkable. Now, and one

00:32:50.389 --> 00:32:57.330
of the things that I could suggest to you,
and I think this is one of the largest ideas

00:32:57.330 --> 00:33:03.340
that exist—especially communicable in this
short of time—is that it’s much better

00:33:03.340 --> 00:33:10.320
to identify with your capacity to transform
than it is to identify with who you are now.

00:33:10.320 --> 00:33:14.029
And that can get you out of an awful lot of
trouble. I mean one of the things that characterizes

00:33:14.029 --> 00:33:18.409
ideologues, for example, or people who are
trapped in fixed belief systems is that they

00:33:18.409 --> 00:33:22.669
worship who they are now. And that’d be
all fine if everything was going a hundred

00:33:22.669 --> 00:33:27.059
percent for them but it’s seldom the case.
And so, unless you’re life is perfect—and

00:33:27.059 --> 00:33:33.240
that seems highly improbable—maybe if you
were someone else it would be better. And

00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:37.760
you know, that’s all you’ve got in some
sense because changing the world in some way

00:33:37.760 --> 00:33:41.450
that’s going to make you feel better about
it, well you know, you might be able to do

00:33:41.450 --> 00:33:46.659
that in some small way but the probability
that you could or should try to do that on

00:33:46.659 --> 00:33:50.100
a large scale is—it’s pretty low.

00:33:50.100 --> 00:33:55.850
So, in the mythological representation section
I’m going to talk to you about the fundamental

00:33:55.850 --> 00:34:02.549
elements of narrative. And the reason that
I think that that’s necessary is because

00:34:02.549 --> 00:34:07.769
there’s sort of two things that you have
to consider when you’re talking about people

00:34:07.769 --> 00:34:14.350
and one is: objectively, what is a person
like? The same way you might think about what

00:34:14.350 --> 00:34:20.100
a rat is like or maybe even what a rock is
like. So, that’s a scientific question in

00:34:20.100 --> 00:34:26.190
some sense. But then there’s another question
which—or maybe two questions: how do people

00:34:26.190 --> 00:34:32.210
act and how should they act? And maybe the
first one is a scientific question but the

00:34:32.210 --> 00:34:38.220
second one certainly isn’t. And the way
that we explore how people do act and how

00:34:38.220 --> 00:34:42.730
they should act has a lot more to do with
narrative than to do with science.

00:34:42.730 --> 00:34:45.790
And you know, you know this; all you have
to do is think about it. You know, you think

00:34:45.790 --> 00:34:50.140
about where do you get most of your information
about what people are like when you’re not

00:34:50.140 --> 00:34:54.730
actually just interacting with some people.
And the answer to that is quite straightforward:

00:34:54.730 --> 00:35:00.140
you read novels, you read literature, you
go see movies, you watch Game of Thrones,

00:35:00.140 --> 00:35:05.190
you binge on Game of Thrones you know, you
go watch Star Wars, you know maybe you line

00:35:05.190 --> 00:35:10.050
up for three days if you’re completely insane
to go see Star Wars. You know and you might

00:35:10.050 --> 00:35:15.300
ask, “Well, what in the world are you doing?”
Well, people are hungry for information about

00:35:15.300 --> 00:35:23.900
how people do act and how they should act.
And we’re absolutely compelled by narratives.

00:35:23.900 --> 00:35:26.980
And there’s a reason for that. Well, and
you kind of know what a narrative is and what

00:35:26.980 --> 00:35:31.290
one isn’t, right? You go to a movie and
you think, “Ah, the character development’s

00:35:31.290 --> 00:35:36.160
pretty good but, you know, as a story it was
terrible.” And then maybe you talk to your

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:39.980
friends about why it was a terrible story
but the funny thing is you kind of know, right?

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:45.520
It’s like, some things are stories and some
things aren’t and laying out what actually

00:35:45.520 --> 00:35:52.540
makes something a story, that’s very difficult.
But you still know it and you can have some consensus

00:35:52.540 --> 00:35:59.070
even. You know, if you look on IMDB or Rotten
Tomatoes, you know, people form large-group

00:35:59.070 --> 00:36:04.570
consensus about what constitutes an acceptable
narrative pretty damn quickly. And there seems

00:36:04.570 --> 00:36:10.410
to be at least a certain amount of agreement,
you know, barring individual differences.

00:36:10.410 --> 00:36:19.580
So, the mythological and the shamanic discussions
that we’re going to undertake have a couple

00:36:19.580 --> 00:36:26.360
of purposes and one is to provide you with
the foundations of a language that you can

00:36:26.360 --> 00:36:32.090
use to understand narrative. And that’s
going to prove very very useful because most

00:36:32.090 --> 00:36:37.630
of the clinical theories that we’re going
to talk about are really narrative based.

00:36:37.630 --> 00:36:42.710
And once you understand their mythological
substructure so to speak, it gives you a framework

00:36:42.710 --> 00:36:47.500
within which you can slot the theories. You
know, because one of the things that’s complicated

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:52.130
about personality theory is that there are
a lot of personality theories. And you might

00:36:52.130 --> 00:36:57.930
think, “Well, how the hell can that be?”
Because if it’s a theory, theoretically

00:36:57.930 --> 00:37:00.900
there can only be one. Like, there’s only
one Theory of Relativity, roughly speaking,

00:37:00.900 --> 00:37:05.740
because it’s a little fuzzy around the edges
but people agree on the core elements. While

00:37:05.740 --> 00:37:12.750
with personality theory, there’s many many
different personality theories. And, so, then

00:37:12.750 --> 00:37:16.030
you might think, “Well, how are you supposed
to make any sense out of that?” And well

00:37:16.030 --> 00:37:21.840
there’s two ways. One is to put of all of
them into a more fundamental underlying structure,

00:37:21.840 --> 00:37:25.020
and that’s something that I’m really going
to strive to have you guys do while you’re

00:37:25.020 --> 00:37:30.100
taking this course. And the other is to actually
adopt a slightly different set of presuppositions

00:37:30.100 --> 00:37:31.620
about personality theories.

00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:36.700
And so you can say, “Well, there’s only
one Theory of Relativity,” but then you

00:37:36.700 --> 00:37:42.610
might say, “Well yeah, there’s only one
Theory of Relativity but then consider a toolbox.”

00:37:42.610 --> 00:37:48.350
So maybe some of you are relatively handy
and you have a toolbox. What’s in a toolbox?

00:37:48.350 --> 00:37:56.860
Hammers—or a hammer—saws, wrenches, pliers,
vise grips, etc. You know and you might say,

00:37:56.860 --> 00:38:04.740
“Well, they’re tools. Why do you need
all those tools?” And the answer is well,

00:38:04.740 --> 00:38:08.410
you can hammer a nail in with a wrench but
it’d be a lot better if you could use a

00:38:08.410 --> 00:38:11.890
hammer and you can probably bang a bolt off
with a hammer but it would be a lot better

00:38:11.890 --> 00:38:16.140
if you have a wrench. And, so, then you might
think, “Well, if you’re trying to do something

00:38:16.140 --> 00:38:22.650
complicated and sophisticated, why not have
a bunch of tools?” Now, we don’t generally

00:38:22.650 --> 00:38:29.360
think of scientific theories as tools, although
that is what they are. Above all else, they’re

00:38:29.360 --> 00:38:30.050
tools.

00:38:30.050 --> 00:38:33.470
And then when we’re dealing with something
as complicated as a human being, well we may

00:38:33.470 --> 00:38:37.640
need more than one tool to get to the bottom
of it. And it’s also the case, you know,

00:38:37.640 --> 00:38:43.120
that to say understand a human being, well
that’s a complicated thing. It’s like—because

00:38:43.120 --> 00:38:48.570
you might say, “Understand to what end?”
You don’t mean comprehensive, universal,

00:38:48.570 --> 00:38:52.740
omniscient understanding. Obviously, you mean,
“I want to know X about someone because

00:38:52.740 --> 00:38:58.670
I have some desire in relationship to my interactions
with them.” And so, there’s all sorts

00:38:58.670 --> 00:39:05.010
of tools that can facilitate that. So what
I would say is that, you know, Freud’s a

00:39:05.010 --> 00:39:10.120
microscope and Jung is a telescope; that’s
one way of thinking about it. I mean there’s

00:39:10.120 --> 00:39:14.440
no particular reason I associated either of
those with those two tools, but the point

00:39:14.440 --> 00:39:20.880
is is that if paid careful attention to personality
theories they can expand your capacity to

00:39:20.880 --> 00:39:26.530
represent yourself in ways that’s extremely
useful. Because, you know, you should understand

00:39:26.530 --> 00:39:33.180
who you are and why, and what implications
that has for you. Because you’re a transformable

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:37.120
creature but you also have a fairly stable
underlying substructure, right? Otherwise

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:43.610
you’d just melt like an octopus. So, you’re
this weird dynamic combination of relatively

00:39:43.610 --> 00:39:50.770
permanent structure and then embedded capacity
to transform. You have a nature and so understanding

00:39:50.770 --> 00:39:55.050
your nature is extraordinarily worthwhile.
And so hopefully what we’re going to do

00:39:55.050 --> 00:39:59.540
during this course is give you a whole bunch
of tools to understand yourself and other

00:39:59.540 --> 00:40:05.130
people with. Tools that will really be useful;
things you can use day to day to understand

00:40:05.130 --> 00:40:10.320
yourselves and your families and so on and
to improve your future—with any luck. And

00:40:10.320 --> 00:40:17.380
then also cover the elements of you that are
stable, most of which I would say have been

00:40:17.380 --> 00:40:23.370
discovered—at least in the more objective
sense—by biological psychologists. So, mostly

00:40:23.370 --> 00:40:25.590
animal researchers peculiarly enough.

00:40:25.590 --> 00:40:30.520
Okay, so mythological representations—we’re
going to take a trip back through time. We’re

00:40:30.520 --> 00:40:37.820
going to look at archaic and ancient stories
of mankind told by members of our species

00:40:37.820 --> 00:40:43.470
to each other. And I’m going to concentrate
on stories that have lasted—we don’t know

00:40:43.470 --> 00:40:49.540
how long. You know, we have stories that in
the written form are 4000 years old, but all

00:40:49.540 --> 00:40:55.390
the evidence suggests that those stories are
based on traditions that go back far far beyond

00:40:55.390 --> 00:41:02.370
that; at least 50 000 years—and maybe way
before that. So we’ll take a look at those

00:41:02.370 --> 00:41:06.330
and that will give us a foundation that we
can use to build on. And then we’re going

00:41:06.330 --> 00:41:13.740
to look at shamanic rituals and descriptions
to get some sense of the archetype of transformation,

00:41:13.740 --> 00:41:18.860
which sort of means, well, what is it that
you have to undergo when you’re changing

00:41:18.860 --> 00:41:22.570
and what are you likely to experience when
that happens? Now I can give you a bit of

00:41:22.570 --> 00:41:30.840
a preview: um, it’s not always the case—maybe
it’s seldom the case—that if you learn

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:37.080
something important that it’s pleasant.
You know, this happens to people all the time.

00:41:37.080 --> 00:41:42.130
So maybe, I don’t know, maybe you had a
recent relationship that went seriously sour.

00:41:42.130 --> 00:41:45.210
And you know maybe it was because you were
going out with a jerk or maybe it’s because

00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:49.260
you’re just not very sophisticated. And
no doubt you’re trying to figure out which

00:41:49.260 --> 00:41:54.130
of those happens to be true while you’re,
you know, considering the relationship. But

00:41:54.130 --> 00:41:58.830
generally what happens to people when they’re
moving towards something and the bottom falls

00:41:58.830 --> 00:42:04.440
out, because something occurs that they didn’t
understand and couldn’t predict, maybe in

00:42:04.440 --> 00:42:08.550
their own behavior—maybe you had an affair
while you were in your relationship and you

00:42:08.550 --> 00:42:14.490
didn’t expect that—well, what happens
is your current model of yourself, and perhaps

00:42:14.490 --> 00:42:22.190
even of the world, is demonstrated as insufficient.
And that rattles you up badly. And then you’re

00:42:22.190 --> 00:42:26.850
rattled up for some good length of time—not
very motivated, somewhat depressed, fairly

00:42:26.850 --> 00:42:31.640
anxious, you sort of fell apart, you don’t
know which way is up. And then maybe if you’re

00:42:31.640 --> 00:42:35.760
lucky you think about it and talk about it
and put yourself back together. And when you

00:42:35.760 --> 00:42:39.280
come out the other side you’re a little
smarter and a little more together than you

00:42:39.280 --> 00:42:40.300
were to begin with.

00:42:40.300 --> 00:42:45.490
Now, if you’re unlucky, you just stay in
the little pit for—people can stay in the

00:42:45.490 --> 00:42:50.430
little pit for a very very long time and that’s
not so good. And so, you know, the process

00:42:50.430 --> 00:42:54.760
of transforming is actually quite dangerous
because it doesn’t necessarily have to be

00:42:54.760 --> 00:43:00.990
completed in a successful manner. But it does
have a pattern and the pattern usually is

00:43:00.990 --> 00:43:05.790
you think you know what you’re doing and
then something happens to demonstrate that

00:43:05.790 --> 00:43:11.140
you actually don’t know what you’re doing.
And that puts you in a spin. And then through

00:43:11.140 --> 00:43:16.600
careful analysis and support and discussion—and
other processes that we don’t really understand

00:43:16.600 --> 00:43:24.030
very well—you come to incorporate the anomalous
knowledge that knocked you on your back, you

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:32.370
reconstruct your personality and your representations
of the world, and then you move ahead, hopefully,

00:43:32.370 --> 00:43:37.230
more than you were before. But it’s an interesting
thing to know, you know. One of the things

00:43:37.230 --> 00:43:41.310
Nietzsche said, again was that you could—you
could tell the character of a human being

00:43:41.310 --> 00:43:46.820
by how much truth they’re willing to tolerate.
Now that’s an interesting phrase, eh, because

00:43:46.820 --> 00:43:50.370
you generally think of people, because they’re
optimistic, they generally think of truth

00:43:50.370 --> 00:43:54.820
as something positive; obviously it’s better
to know the truth. It’s like, no, if you

00:43:54.820 --> 00:43:59.180
pull the wool over your eyes as a general
rule learning the truth is not a pleasant

00:43:59.180 --> 00:44:02.630
experience. That doesn’t necessarily mean
that it’s something—not something you

00:44:02.630 --> 00:44:12.050
should do, but the idea that the path to enlightenment
is blissful: that’s not a very smart idea.

00:44:12.050 --> 00:44:14.820
In fact, it’s quite the opposite.

00:44:14.820 --> 00:44:20.730
So, the shamanic rituals basically manifest
in an archetypal way. So, one of the things

00:44:20.730 --> 00:44:26.260
you see happening with the typical—the shaman,
by the way shamanism is a worldwide phenomena

00:44:26.260 --> 00:44:32.990
and it seems to be associated with primordial
or archaic psychology of transformation. It’s

00:44:32.990 --> 00:44:40.340
often drug-aided. And generally what happens
is that the shamanic transformation is accompanied

00:44:40.340 --> 00:44:46.750
by the descent into something approximating
psychological death and then a rebuilding

00:44:46.750 --> 00:44:51.020
and a rebirth that puts the person out on
the other side. It’s like an initiation

00:44:51.020 --> 00:44:55.120
ritual and that’s another way of thinking
about it. Or you might think about it as the

00:44:55.120 --> 00:45:02.390
depressing that possessed you during your
first semester of university which either

00:45:02.390 --> 00:45:06.140
knocked you out of the university altogether—because
that happens to a lot of people—or which

00:45:06.140 --> 00:45:11.100
you recovered from and finally became a university
student, although perhaps that wasn’t a

00:45:11.100 --> 00:45:15.330
particularly pleasant transition. You know,
we’ve done some testing with U of T students

00:45:15.330 --> 00:45:19.500
and it’s interesting to note that about
a third of you meet criteria for clinical

00:45:19.500 --> 00:45:24.120
depression your first year here. Now what
that indicates to me is that the criteria

00:45:24.120 --> 00:45:29.240
for clinical depression are a little on the
sensitive side, but regardless—because if

00:45:29.240 --> 00:45:33.530
a third of people meet the criteria you think,
“Yeah, well that’s a little sensitive.”

00:45:33.530 --> 00:45:40.350
But by the same token, you know, it’s not
an easy adjustment and the old you has die.

00:45:40.350 --> 00:45:45.380
And generally that’s not very pleasant.
So, and it’s a good way to think about the

00:45:45.380 --> 00:45:49.260
impediments to learning because you hardly
ever learn something without having part of

00:45:49.260 --> 00:45:53.980
you die first. And that’s part of the reason
that people are rather resistant to learning

00:45:53.980 --> 00:45:55.070
anything new that’s important.

00:45:55.070 --> 00:46:00.710
Okay, well so you’ve got that. Now, after
that we’re going to talk about Piaget. And

00:46:00.710 --> 00:46:09.480
Piaget isn’t necessarily known as a personality
psychologist but that’s okay. Piaget published

00:46:09.480 --> 00:46:14.840
a very—he was a developmental psychologist,
maybe the greatest developmental psychologist.

00:46:14.840 --> 00:46:20.520
He published a very large number of books—many
of which haven’t even been translated yet,

00:46:20.520 --> 00:46:24.590
some of which haven’t even been published
yet. So, Piaget thought a lot of things about

00:46:24.590 --> 00:46:29.020
a lot of things. And he’s generally known
for his stage theory of developmental but

00:46:29.020 --> 00:46:32.830
he really wasn’t all that into that stage
theory. It just seemed to be the part of his

00:46:32.830 --> 00:46:39.230
theory that I would say rather dimwitted Western
intellectuals could grasp onto most easily

00:46:39.230 --> 00:46:44.690
and so that’s generally what you hear about.
But Piaget was a very very sophisticated thinker.

00:46:44.690 --> 00:46:50.370
And one of the things he was interested in
was, well he was interested in how the child

00:46:50.370 --> 00:46:54.380
in some sense boots itself up. That’s a
reasonable way of thinking about it, because

00:46:54.380 --> 00:47:00.090
you know when you turn on a computer, what
happens is a certain number of primitive functions—primitive

00:47:00.090 --> 00:47:04.820
fundamental functions—come online first.
And then those functions enable more complex

00:47:04.820 --> 00:47:10.260
functions to emerge. And then those functions
enable more complex functions to emerge. And

00:47:10.260 --> 00:47:16.820
soon there your computer is, waiting for you.
And human beings are kind of like that. So

00:47:16.820 --> 00:47:22.660
when we’re first born—we’re very primitive
when we’re first born. We’re very immature;

00:47:22.660 --> 00:47:25.750
that’s a better way of thinking about it.
We’re not precisely primitive; we still

00:47:25.750 --> 00:47:29.670
have very large brains and there are more
connections in those brains at birth than

00:47:29.670 --> 00:47:34.970
there will be for the rest of your life, which
is quite interesting. You actually die into

00:47:34.970 --> 00:47:39.500
your personality. Because what happens isn’t
so much that you make new connections; it’s

00:47:39.500 --> 00:47:44.520
that a whole bunch of the ones you already
have that you don’t use disappear. So that’s

00:47:44.520 --> 00:47:45.980
pretty peculiar.

00:47:45.980 --> 00:47:51.290
Anyways, when you’re first born there’s
not a lot to you in terms of capacity to engage

00:47:51.290 --> 00:47:55.410
with the world. Your moth is pretty wired
up, your lips and your tongue—which is a

00:47:55.410 --> 00:47:59.620
good thing because you have to breastfeed
and that’s actually not very simple. You

00:47:59.620 --> 00:48:03.830
have to have a clue to do that and it’s
the first complex social interaction that

00:48:03.830 --> 00:48:10.010
you’re likely to engage in. So, you—you’re
born with a bit of a priori sophisticated

00:48:10.010 --> 00:48:15.920
wiring already in place, but the rest of you,
man, it’s not there much at all. You can’t

00:48:15.920 --> 00:48:20.120
control your arms; you know if you watch a
baby in a crib—a young baby—and it’s

00:48:20.120 --> 00:48:24.150
not swaddled up, you know their arms sort
of float around in space and so do their legs.

00:48:24.150 --> 00:48:27.460
And now and then they might hit themselves
and generally that seems to come as quite

00:48:27.460 --> 00:48:34.470
a shock to the baby. It’s not—its peripheral
nervous system, the peripheral elements of

00:48:34.470 --> 00:48:40.070
its central nervous system are not well developed
at birth. And so it’s almost as if the baby

00:48:40.070 --> 00:48:45.620
has to discover its limbs—it sort of expands
from the middle outwards—it has to discover

00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:50.070
its limbs and then start to figure out what
to do with them.

00:48:50.070 --> 00:48:57.050
And so what Piaget attempted to do was to
lay out the processes by which a child unfolded

00:48:57.050 --> 00:49:02.930
into the world. Now, some of—there’s a
biological basis to that because you come

00:49:02.930 --> 00:49:08.870
into the world at birth with a set of reflexes
at hand. And those reflexes are enough to

00:49:08.870 --> 00:49:16.010
get you started. And so Piaget—here’s
one way of trying to understand Piaget. I

00:49:16.010 --> 00:49:20.940
like it quite a bit. It’s a bit of an oversimplification
but it’ll do. There’s lots of different

00:49:20.940 --> 00:49:25.420
ways of thinking about what the world is made
of. Now, you know, if you’re classically—if

00:49:25.420 --> 00:49:29.510
you’re a classical scientist—you tend
to think that the world’s made out of matter.

00:49:29.510 --> 00:49:34.210
You know and then matter is made out of atoms
and then atoms are made of—god only knows

00:49:34.210 --> 00:49:38.330
what atoms are made out of, right, because
by the time you get under atoms you just have

00:49:38.330 --> 00:49:42.280
no idea what’s going on down there. But,
you know, we generally don’t think about

00:49:42.280 --> 00:49:47.150
that too much and we sort of assume Newtonian
reality and that everything is made out of

00:49:47.150 --> 00:49:51.050
hard little particles like dust and that’s
the scientific model.

00:49:51.050 --> 00:49:56.830
But there’s other ways of looking at the
world that are not only equally valid but

00:49:56.830 --> 00:50:01.550
equally necessary and equally powerful. One
of them is that you can look at the world

00:50:01.550 --> 00:50:07.510
as a matrix of information. You know, and
so information generally has significance

00:50:07.510 --> 00:50:12.670
and meaning—at least insofar as information
is conceptualized by human beings. And so,

00:50:12.670 --> 00:50:16.650
for example, if you’re ten years old and
you’re crawling around on the floor and

00:50:16.650 --> 00:50:23.730
the dog has a ball and you grab it and the
dog nips you, that’s informative. And so,

00:50:23.730 --> 00:50:31.180
what Piaget claims is that children come into
the world with an information-seeking structure

00:50:31.180 --> 00:50:38.440
and that as they interact with the world phenomena
emerge that they can model with their bodies.

00:50:38.440 --> 00:50:46.070
So, for example, if I’m going to drink this
then I have to model it with my hand before

00:50:46.070 --> 00:50:50.730
I can do so. And so it turns out, for example,
when if this thing’s sitting here—especially

00:50:50.730 --> 00:50:57.310
if I’m thirsty—and I look at it my eyes
tell my hands to get ready to grab it. And

00:50:57.310 --> 00:51:04.120
so when you say you understand something what
you mean is that—part of what you mean—is

00:51:04.120 --> 00:51:10.620
that when you perceive it, it maps itself
onto your body. And so this happens at pretty

00:51:10.620 --> 00:51:12.040
low levels of your nervous system.

00:51:12.040 --> 00:51:17.750
So, for example, there are people who are
blind. They say they’re blind; they can’t

00:51:17.750 --> 00:51:23.520
see anything. But if you move your hands up
like this and ask them to guess which hand

00:51:23.520 --> 00:51:27.960
you’re lifting they can do it with almost
a hundred percent accuracy. And you think,

00:51:27.960 --> 00:51:32.830
“Well how the hell can that possibly be?
They’re blind!” Well, the reason that

00:51:32.830 --> 00:51:38.520
happens is because your eyes are detecting
patterns in the world, some of those patterns

00:51:38.520 --> 00:51:45.780
you perceive as conscious reality, but other
patterns are just mapped onto your spine or

00:51:45.780 --> 00:51:49.690
onto your emotional system. Doesn’t—you
don’t need the perception of the object

00:51:49.690 --> 00:51:52.850
in order to have that activated. Because,
you know, you probably think, “There’s

00:51:52.850 --> 00:51:56.480
the world,” you see it, you think about
it, you evaluate it, and you act. It’s like,

00:51:56.480 --> 00:52:02.000
that isn’t how it works. It’s very seldomly
how it works. Partly because it’s just too

00:52:02.000 --> 00:52:06.410
damn slow. You know, if you had to think about
everything you’d be running over pedestrians

00:52:06.410 --> 00:52:10.490
nonstop, right? You have to be able to react
very very quickly and part of the way you

00:52:10.490 --> 00:52:17.960
do that is by having your sensory systems
map right onto your motor output systems.

00:52:17.960 --> 00:52:30.450
So, Piaget. Well, for Piaget, part of the way
that the personality emerges out of the doldrums

00:52:30.450 --> 00:52:36.620
of infancy is that the child encounters the
world and incorporates the information that

00:52:36.620 --> 00:52:41.310
they gather as a consequence of bumping up
against it. And, then, they build themselves

00:52:41.310 --> 00:52:46.530
in some sense from their bodies and the ability—the
abilities that they’re practicing with their

00:52:46.530 --> 00:52:50.510
bodies like the gripping mechanism and the
pointing mechanism and the scratching mechanism

00:52:50.510 --> 00:52:54.700
and all those things that they can build—they
build those up to the point where the can

00:52:54.700 --> 00:53:00.210
start to use abstractions. And so the baby
sort of spirals out of nowhere using its exploratory

00:53:00.210 --> 00:53:05.080
capacity, absorbing information from the world,
and creates its personality that way. It’s

00:53:05.080 --> 00:53:08.910
a lovely model and we’ll cover that in some
detail.

00:53:08.910 --> 00:53:15.300
The depth psychologists. I concentrate primarily
on Jung and Freud. And you know if you take

00:53:15.300 --> 00:53:20.580
a personality course, generally you’ll be
introduced to Freud first and then you’ll

00:53:20.580 --> 00:53:24.420
be introduced to Jung and maybe Adler and
some of the neo-Freudians, more or less as

00:53:24.420 --> 00:53:32.780
afterthoughts to Freud. But I don’t really
think that’s appropriate. First of all,

00:53:32.780 --> 00:53:41.450
I think you can make a strong case that Jung
was—that the utility of Jung’s theory

00:53:41.450 --> 00:53:48.460
is particularly evident when you’re talking
about radical similarities between people.

00:53:48.460 --> 00:53:55.910
So, Jung was very biologically orientated.
In some ways he was way ahead of biological

00:53:55.910 --> 00:54:02.180
psychiatrists and psychologists because when
Jung was beginning to formulate his theories,

00:54:02.180 --> 00:54:07.570
in the West—in North America anyways and
in the United States—most of the people

00:54:07.570 --> 00:54:11.720
who were studying human beings were behaviorists.
And behaviorists basically presume that you

00:54:11.720 --> 00:54:16.840
were a blank slate at birth and that you learned
how to do everything that you know how to

00:54:16.840 --> 00:54:22.980
do. Well, that’s not true at all.

00:54:22.980 --> 00:54:27.090
First of all, you can’t learn everything
that you know how to do because you have to

00:54:27.090 --> 00:54:32.400
know something to start the whole process
off, and second, you know, you don’t have

00:54:32.400 --> 00:54:36.610
to learn really to understand what a dog’s
growl means. You know it’s kind of wired

00:54:36.610 --> 00:54:40.740
into you. And you really don’t have to learn
to be afraid of the dark, man. You’ve got

00:54:40.740 --> 00:54:44.520
that down by the time you’re about three.
You know, so there’s lots of things that

00:54:44.520 --> 00:54:48.340
are built right into people and what exactly
that means we’re not sure about. I mean

00:54:48.340 --> 00:54:54.350
it does look, for example, like snake phobia
is innate and even if the actual phobia

00:54:54.350 --> 00:54:58.260
isn’t innate—and it probably is—you
can learn to be phobic of snakes in no time

00:54:58.260 --> 00:55:02.900
flat, whereas like trying to become phobic
of pistols is very very difficult.

00:55:02.900 --> 00:55:07.920
Now, Jung was very interested in the—I think
what the best way to think about it is the

00:55:07.920 --> 00:55:13.330
substrata of human cognition. You know, because
human beings are a particular type of thing.

00:55:13.330 --> 00:55:19.960
It’s not like our souls are disconnected
from our bodies and that our knowledge is

00:55:19.960 --> 00:55:27.970
somehow appropriate or universal in some way
that would make sense if we didn’t have

00:55:27.970 --> 00:55:34.300
bodies. We’re not like that at all. We’re
in our bodies. Our brains are adapted to our

00:55:34.300 --> 00:55:39.790
bodies. Our minds are adapted to our bodies
and so is our knowledge. And that means that

00:55:39.790 --> 00:55:45.850
our cognitive abilities—our theories of
the world—have biological substructures

00:55:45.850 --> 00:55:50.450
and those are presuppositions in some sense
for our knowledge that we don’t have to

00:55:50.450 --> 00:55:55.010
learn. So, for example, you don’t really
have to learn that being punched hurts; you

00:55:55.010 --> 00:55:59.340
just discover that. You don’t really have
to learn what a smile means. You don’t really

00:55:59.340 --> 00:56:04.080
have to learn what anger means. You know,
you’ll experience it. So it’s right in

00:56:04.080 --> 00:56:08.860
you. You watch little kids, little babies—when
they’re crying they’re often angry. So,

00:56:08.860 --> 00:56:17.000
for example, by nine months of age a baby
can more or less identify its mother. So, someone

00:56:17.000 --> 00:56:21.050
walks into the baby’s room and takes a look
at the baby and it’s not the baby’s mother

00:56:21.050 --> 00:56:24.710
and the baby starts crying and you think,
“Oh, poor baby.” It’s like, fair enough,

00:56:24.710 --> 00:56:30.240
that baby is not sad; that baby is angry.
And you can tell if you look at the baby because

00:56:30.240 --> 00:56:34.520
it’s turning all red and that’s what people
do when they cry when they’re angry. And

00:56:34.520 --> 00:56:37.800
so it’s a good thing to know when you’re
arguing with someone and they get upset and

00:56:37.800 --> 00:56:42.490
maybe they start to cry. You think, “Oh,
the poor person,” but then if you look you’ll

00:56:42.490 --> 00:56:46.190
see that they’re all red. And that means
that they’d like to strangle you but their,

00:56:46.190 --> 00:56:50.670
you know, their social niceties is hopefully
preventing them. So, and that can certainly

00:56:50.670 --> 00:56:55.330
change your attitude towards whether or not
you should be feeling sorry for them when

00:56:55.330 --> 00:56:56.290
they hurt their feelings.

00:56:56.290 --> 00:57:00.920
So, there’s all sorts of things that are
universal about human beings and Jung was

00:57:00.920 --> 00:57:05.550
very very interested in what those universals
are and how they played out in patterns of

00:57:05.550 --> 00:57:13.530
behavior. So, you can imagine—you can imagine
that you have an instinct, say like anger.

00:57:13.530 --> 00:57:16.480
And thinking about it that way, it’s like
a cross-section: that’s person’s angry.

00:57:16.480 --> 00:57:21.030
You know, and you can imagine the facial configuration
that would go along with that. But in a more

00:57:21.030 --> 00:57:25.890
sophisticated way if you wanted to represent
anger maybe you’d represent it like a drama—like

00:57:25.890 --> 00:57:32.850
a story. You know, and that’s actually what
you want to know if someone gets angry, right?

00:57:32.850 --> 00:57:38.430
“What happened?” Well, imagine if you
took a thousand stories about angry people

00:57:38.430 --> 00:57:43.360
and you boiled them all into one story so
that everything that was common about all

00:57:43.360 --> 00:57:48.010
the stories about anger were encapsulated
in a single story. Well, that would be the

00:57:48.010 --> 00:57:53.410
archetypal story of anger. And everyone would
recognize it because it’s like anger represented

00:57:53.410 --> 00:57:57.990
like a movie in some sense in it’s purest
form. Well that’s an archetype and that’s

00:57:57.990 --> 00:58:01.740
the sort of thing that Jung was extraordinarily
interested in—understanding and documenting.

00:58:01.740 --> 00:58:05.800
And it’s a tremendous amount of fun. So
one of the things we’ll do is I’m going

00:58:05.800 --> 00:58:09.750
to show you some movies—probably the Lion
King, at least parts of it—and I’m going

00:58:09.750 --> 00:58:14.520
to show you how the archetypal structure manifests
itself. And that’s really worth knowing.

00:58:14.520 --> 00:58:18.660
It’s kind of a pain because then from now
on you’ll go to movies and you’ll think,

00:58:18.660 --> 00:58:21.500
“Oh well, that’s that archetypal structure,”
and “That’s what that symbol means,”

00:58:21.500 --> 00:58:27.120
and you’ll really be annoying to your friends.
But it’s very entertaining and it’s very

00:58:27.120 --> 00:58:31.320
illuminating. And partly because what’s
really cool about these archetypal story structures

00:58:31.320 --> 00:58:36.150
is not only do they tell you who they are
but they tell you in some sense who you should

00:58:36.150 --> 00:58:37.160
be.

00:58:37.160 --> 00:58:40.720
Now, that’s cool, because one of the things
that modern people have a real problem with

00:58:40.720 --> 00:58:45.630
is trying to understand whether or not life
has any meaning, you know. We look at the

00:58:45.630 --> 00:58:49.660
world from a scientific perspective and we
say, “Well, it appears that all this is,

00:58:49.660 --> 00:58:53.690
you know, without meaning; it doesn’t mean
anything with any cosmic sense.” It’s

00:58:53.690 --> 00:58:59.960
a pretty dopey conclusion as far as I’m
concerned because the originators of the scientific

00:58:59.960 --> 00:59:06.570
method excluded subjective meaning from the
doctrine at the outset. So, you know, if you

00:59:06.570 --> 00:59:09.950
look at the world scientifically and you say,
“Well, it’s without subjective meaning,”

00:59:09.950 --> 00:59:14.670
it’s like, “Well, yeah, obviously,” because
science was set up so that all the subjective

00:59:14.670 --> 00:59:18.790
meaning would be stripped away from the phenomena.
So, you know, it’s not something to be really

00:59:18.790 --> 00:59:23.020
shocked about afterwards and it also doesn’t
mean that there’s no such thing as subjective

00:59:23.020 --> 00:59:27.290
meaning. It just means that it’s very difficult
to get a grip on from a purely scientific

00:59:27.290 --> 00:59:28.250
perspective.

00:59:28.250 --> 00:59:34.510
And one of the lovely things about archetypal
stories is they tell you in a way that’s

00:59:34.510 --> 00:59:41.750
universally believable who you are. And that’s
very cool. And, you know, because you think—you

00:59:41.750 --> 00:59:48.050
learn this in university too and it just tears
me up, you know. You learn implicitly or explicitly

00:59:48.050 --> 00:59:51.450
in almost all of the fields of study that
you’re going to undertake—especially on

00:59:51.450 --> 00:59:57.030
the humanities end—that morality is relative
and that there’s no ultimate meaning, which

00:59:57.030 --> 01:00:02.390
is a really rotten thing to be teaching people
of your age. You know, because you’re too

01:00:02.390 --> 01:00:06.920
damn young to be nihilistic. You know, you
can be nihilistic when you’re 85—you’re

01:00:06.920 --> 01:00:11.590
all done by then—but to be nihilistic now
that’s a bloody catastrophe. You know, and

01:00:11.590 --> 01:00:16.910
it’s completely pointless because the notion
of—the notion that things are meaningless

01:00:16.910 --> 01:00:25.130
in some ultimate sense: it’s an extraordinarily
primitive philosophical theory. And it does

01:00:25.130 --> 01:00:31.400
not serve people well. It makes them sick.
And so one of the things that you might consider

01:00:31.400 --> 01:00:35.940
is that if your stupid theory makes you sick
maybe there’s something wrong with it. It

01:00:35.940 --> 01:00:43.090
might be a criteria for the utility—or perhaps
even the truth of the theory, you know. Depends

01:00:43.090 --> 01:00:48.130
on how you think about these things. You know,
you might say, “Well, if a truth makes you

01:00:48.130 --> 01:00:52.570
sick that doesn’t mean it’s any less of
a truth.” Well, maybe. Maybe it really does

01:00:52.570 --> 01:00:57.240
mean that it’s much much less of a truth.
It depends to some degree what you mean by

01:00:57.240 --> 01:00:58.850
truth.

01:00:58.850 --> 01:01:04.780
So, anyways, Jung’s very very very very useful
for that. He’s a terrifying thinker. Really,

01:01:04.780 --> 01:01:11.530
like I read—when I was about 23 or 24 I
spent two years and I read pretty much about

01:01:11.530 --> 01:01:16.660
everything Jung wrote; it’s about twenty-three
volumes. And it just blew me into bits. I

01:01:16.660 --> 01:01:22.360
had no idea what the hell was going on—my
dreams changed. Really, it was really rough.

01:01:22.360 --> 01:01:26.940
I was reading Nietzsche at the same time and
he’s just about as bad. Like Nietzsche said

01:01:26.940 --> 01:01:30.550
he philosophized with a hammer. You know,
and that’s great unless he’s taking a

01:01:30.550 --> 01:01:35.910
hammer to your skull and that’s not nearly
so pleasant. So, you know, Jung—he’s a

01:01:35.910 --> 01:01:38.580
completely different sort of creature and
I think that’s part of the reason that you

01:01:38.580 --> 01:01:42.600
never really learn about him in university.
You know, if you don’t understand him he

01:01:42.600 --> 01:01:46.040
looks weird, and if you do understand him
a little bit he’s terrifying and you’re

01:01:46.040 --> 01:01:49.840
bloody well going to stop. You’ll think,
“Ah, no that’s okay. I don’t—I don’t

01:01:49.840 --> 01:01:51.430
really need to know that.”

01:01:51.430 --> 01:02:00.720
So, Freud. Freud’s a nice whipping boy for
psychologists, which I also think is pretty

01:02:00.720 --> 01:02:05.020
pathetic because ungrateful to begin with.
You know, Freud was really the person who

01:02:05.020 --> 01:02:09.430
formalized the idea of the unconscious. You
know, and everyone takes that for granted,

01:02:09.430 --> 01:02:14.170
that there’s a cognitive unconscious. That’s
what the psychologists discovered in what,

01:02:14.170 --> 01:02:17.840
the 1980’s. “There’s a cognitive unconscious.”
It’s like, “Yeah, good work guys. They’d

01:02:17.840 --> 01:02:23.150
figured that out back in 1910.” You know,
but you don’t see credit being given to

01:02:23.150 --> 01:02:26.950
Freud for making that sort of discovery if
you look at experimental psychology, which

01:02:26.950 --> 01:02:29.450
I think—like I said—I think that’s really
ungrateful.

01:02:29.450 --> 01:02:36.310
Freud’s a strange guy. And, you know, you
can take issue with—especially the details

01:02:36.310 --> 01:02:40.430
of his theory. You know, like the Oedipal
complex, for example, or penis envy, those

01:02:40.430 --> 01:02:47.000
sorts of things. It’s like, you know, in
some ways they seem laughably anachronistic,

01:02:47.000 --> 01:02:54.560
but you know sometimes someone can be wrong
in the details and right in the overall conception.

01:02:54.560 --> 01:02:58.390
You know, because theories are multilayered
things, right? So, sometimes I read a student

01:02:58.390 --> 01:03:03.700
essay, you know, and the sentences are just
awful. This person, like God only knows where

01:03:03.700 --> 01:03:10.700
they learned to write—probably in a high
school somewhere. And, you know, it’s incomprehensible,

01:03:10.700 --> 01:03:13.930
but if you read the whole thing you think,
“Man, there’s an idea in here.” You

01:03:13.930 --> 01:03:17.750
know, the person is smarter than their ability
to write. And so then you can write—you

01:03:17.750 --> 01:03:21.680
can say, “Look, you can’t write worth
a damn but, you know, you’re pretty smart

01:03:21.680 --> 01:03:25.750
and you’ve got some ideas in here that if
you could articulate might be of really high

01:03:25.750 --> 01:03:29.720
quality.” So, you can be wrong in the details
and right in the overall picture and Freud

01:03:29.720 --> 01:03:31.270
was like that all the time.

01:03:31.270 --> 01:03:45.450
And Freud was also unbeatable in relationship
to familial pathology. So, from Freud’s

01:03:45.450 --> 01:03:53.220
perspective, the fundamental task of the emerging
human being was to extricate themselves from

01:03:53.220 --> 01:03:57.480
their family. Now, you might think, “Well
why is that such a problem?” Well, here’s

01:03:57.480 --> 01:04:02.750
a couple of reasons. Well, first, your parents
take care of you so like maybe you don’t

01:04:02.750 --> 01:04:07.910
want to dispense with that too early, especially
if they really really take care of you. And

01:04:07.910 --> 01:04:11.880
that’s the Oedipal situation from the Freudian
perspective. It’s like, the Oedipal’s

01:04:11.880 --> 01:04:18.210
situation arises when you have a parent—or
two parents—who do so much for you that

01:04:18.210 --> 01:04:23.350
you’re no longer able to do anything for
yourself. And that is not a good situation;

01:04:23.350 --> 01:04:28.550
it’s extraordinarily pathological. And there
is nobody like Freud who lays out the details

01:04:28.550 --> 01:04:32.300
of that pathology. And we’re going to watch
a movie, “Crumb” it’s called, which

01:04:32.300 --> 01:04:36.520
is a very disturbing movie—which I suppose
is a trigger warning for those of you who

01:04:36.520 --> 01:04:41.490
think you need trigger warnings. It’s a
very very distressing documentary but it will

01:04:41.490 --> 01:04:46.970
show you Freudian psychopathology like you’ve
never seen it—unless you happen to live

01:04:46.970 --> 01:04:49.330
in a thoroughly Oedipal family.

01:04:49.330 --> 01:04:54.710
Alright. So, look, I didn’t get through the
whole outline. I’ll tell you very briefly

01:04:54.710 --> 01:04:59.430
what you’re going to be required to do and
then we’ll continue this is the next lecture.

01:04:59.430 --> 01:05:05.060
So, there’s three exams: two midterms and
a final. They’re multiple choice. Okay,

01:05:05.060 --> 01:05:08.680
I’ll post some of the questions before the
exam so that you know what you’re getting

01:05:08.680 --> 01:05:13.520
into. I don’t think they’re particularly
difficult; some of you will beg to differ.

01:05:13.520 --> 01:05:18.200
There’s pressure on us this year to curve
the grades so beware. I may have to do that

01:05:18.200 --> 01:05:22.880
okay just—and you need to know that. You
have to write an essay: 750 words. That’s

01:05:22.880 --> 01:05:29.140
not a very long essay but that makes it actually
more difficult. The sign-up for the topics

01:05:29.140 --> 01:05:34.070
of the essay are on the website. You can just
go there and sign up. There’s twenty different

01:05:34.070 --> 01:05:38.220
topics and there’s fifteen slots for each
topic so I would recommend that you do that

01:05:38.220 --> 01:05:44.680
quite quickly. You have to do a personality
self-analysis: 7.5 percent of your grade.

01:05:44.680 --> 01:05:52.310
The link to that is online too. You can get
started on that whenever you want.

01:05:52.310 --> 01:05:58.000
Your TA—one of them—that’s Christine
Brophy; she’s over there in the corner patiently

01:05:58.000 --> 01:06:03.780
waiting for me to introduce her. She has office
hours; they’re posted online. You can sign

01:06:03.780 --> 01:06:09.830
up for her office hours online. You can also
sign up for my office hours online. So, well

01:06:09.830 --> 01:06:15.560
that’s halfway through what the course is
going to be about. So, we’ll see you on Thursday.

